          body: "The capital cost of a non-current asset is not only the price on the invoice. It includes every cost that is needed to get the asset to the place where it will be used and to make it ready to work. For a machine this means delivery, a concrete base, wiring and installation, and testing before the first use. For a building it includes legal fees and any alterations needed before it can be occupied. For a vehicle it includes sign-writing or a delivery box fitted when the vehicle is bought.\n\nCosts that happen after the asset is working are revenue expenditure: fuel, insurance, road tax, servicing, repairs and the wages of the operator. Staff training is also revenue expenditure, because the knowledge belongs to the person and not to the asset. The table shows typical items. When you meet a new item in a question, ask one question: does this payment create the asset or improve it, or does it just keep the asset running?",
