          heading: "Capital receipts and revenue receipts",
          body: "Receipts are classified in the same way as payments. A revenue receipt is income earned from the normal activities of the business. For a shop this is sales; for a tutoring centre it is fees; for any business it also includes rent received, commission received and interest received. Revenue receipts are credited to income accounts and appear in the statement of profit or loss.\n\nA capital receipt does not come from trading. The three common examples are capital introduced by the owner, a loan received, and the money received when a non-current asset is sold. None of these is income. Capital introduced increases the capital account. A loan increases a liability. The proceeds of selling an asset go to the disposal account, and only the difference between the proceeds and the carrying amount is a profit or loss on disposal. A business that credits a $10,000 loan to sales will show a profit that is $10,000 too high and will leave a $10,000 liability out of the statement of financial position.",
