          heading: "Common problems in the inventory count",
          body: "Questions often describe mistakes made during the count and ask you to correct the figure. Goods valued at selling price must be converted back to cost: if the mark-up is 25 per cent, cost is selling price divided by 1.25. Goods left out of the count, perhaps stored in a van or a second storeroom, must be added at cost. Damaged or obsolete goods counted at cost must be reduced to their NRV. Goods that belong to a customer and are only being stored, or goods already sold but not yet collected, must be taken out. Goods bought and received before the year end must be included even if the invoice has not yet arrived.\n\nWork through each item separately and write the adjustment with a plus or minus sign before you touch the total. Then recalculate gross profit if the question asks for it. Remember that a change in closing inventory changes gross profit by exactly the same amount in the same direction: inventory up $300 means gross profit up $300.",
